WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.592 --> 00:00:02.968
Into the motor.

2
00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:05.796
Lecture six, the last on the hairpin stator.

3
00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:13.944
Lecture five left our EV-size stator, 48 slots and 8 poles, impregnated and tested:

4
00:00:13.944 --> 00:00:19.032
9.17 milliohms between two leads, and its insulation proven.

5
00:00:19.032 --> 00:00:20.484
Now it becomes a motor.

6
00:00:21.272 --> 00:00:22.456
Five steps:

7
00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:28.440
housing, cooling, connections, rotor, and a last test of the whole motor.

8
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.968
Beside it, wherever it differs:

9
00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:37.209
our small 24-slot core in a finned industrial frame, an IEC frame, built as

10
00:00:37.209 --> 00:00:38.340
an induction motor.

11
00:00:39.064 --> 00:00:43.576
The numbers come from our Hairpin Motor Designer's model of that stator.

12
00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:48.376
It has no housing or rotor yet, so we sized both from published motors

13
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:49.912
and assumed the rest;

14
00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:51.076
we say so each time.

15
00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:53.301
Chapter one.

16
00:00:53.301 --> 00:00:54.241
Into the housing.

17
00:00:55.825 --> 00:01:00.234
The housing must hold the stator for the car's life, carry its torque and

18
00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.841
take its heat.

19
00:01:01.841 --> 00:01:06.550
In battery cars the core is usually pressed into an aluminium housing with a

20
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:07.857
water jacket;

21
00:01:07.857 --> 00:01:10.589
several hybrid motors bolt it in instead.

22
00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:14.097
The usual press is a shrink fit.

23
00:01:14.097 --> 00:01:17.489
The housing's bore is made slightly smaller than the core;

24
00:01:17.489 --> 00:01:20.529
the difference is called the interference.

25
00:01:20.529 --> 00:01:23.153
Heated, the aluminium grows;

26
00:01:23.153 --> 00:01:25.041
the cold core drops in;

27
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:27.197
as it cools, the housing grips it.

28
00:01:28.305 --> 00:01:30.833
How much grip does the torque need?

29
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:33.521
Friction at the fit must hold it.

30
00:01:33.521 --> 00:01:39.441
Our estimated peak torque, for an assumed rotor, is 260 newton-metres.

31
00:01:39.441 --> 00:01:45.188
Doubled for safety, with a friction coefficient of 0.15, that needs under half a

32
00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:46.449
megapascal:

33
00:01:46.449 --> 00:01:51.101
0.04 millimetres of interference on our 200-millimetre core.

34
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:56.145
Yet the few published fits we found are several times larger:

35
00:01:56.145 --> 00:02:02.033
scaled to our size, 0.12 to 0.26 millimetres.

36
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.049
The reason is heat.

37
00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.440
Aluminium expands almost twice as much as steel, so when both warm, the fit

38
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.189
loosens by 1.9 micrometres a kelvin.

39
00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:16.625
So the fit is sized for heat, not torque.

40
00:02:16.625 --> 00:02:21.649
We chose one that still grips with both parts soaked at 120 degrees:

41
00:02:21.649 --> 00:02:25.713
0.23 to 0.28 millimetres.

42
00:02:25.713 --> 00:02:31.331
With a 4-millimetre aluminium wall, as in published designs, it presses about 3 megapascals

43
00:02:31.331 --> 00:02:32.509
at room temperature.

44
00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:37.429
In the car, the jacket keeps the housing cooler than the core, and the

45
00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:39.569
grip stays as assembled.

46
00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:42.961
The weak moment is a hot soak with the coolant stopped:

47
00:02:42.961 --> 00:02:47.951
in our model, the smallest fit lets go when both parts reach about 140

48
00:02:47.951 --> 00:02:48.751
degrees.

49
00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:55.537
To assemble, the bore must open by the interference plus about 0.2 millimetres:

50
00:02:55.537 --> 00:03:00.657
in our model the housing must reach at least 131 degrees.

51
00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:05.373
Makers of induction heaters quote 200 to 300, in a minute or two.

52
00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:08.433
Could that heat harm the winding?

53
00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:10.609
We found no published limit.

54
00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:16.490
Shared evenly with core and copper, a 200-degree housing would leave them near 110

55
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:20.945
degrees, a 300-degree one near 160;

56
00:03:20.945 --> 00:03:22.973
the core's outside gets hotter first.

57
00:03:23.825 --> 00:03:25.425
The core pays too.

58
00:03:25.425 --> 00:03:29.297
The fit squeezes the yoke, the steel ring behind the slots:

59
00:03:29.297 --> 00:03:36.177
in our estimate to 25 to 31 megapascals, inside the 16 to 48 published.

60
00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:38.449
Compressed steel loses more:

61
00:03:38.449 --> 00:03:44.513
one thesis measured 18 percent more at 10 megapascals, and in its 48-slot, 8-pole

62
00:03:44.513 --> 00:03:47.485
model, half a point of drive-cycle efficiency.

63
00:03:52.661 --> 00:03:53.845
Chapter two.

64
00:03:53.845 --> 00:03:54.645
Cooling.

65
00:03:55.793 --> 00:04:02.802
At 10 amps per square millimetre and 150 degrees, our winding makes 1.67 kilowatts

66
00:04:02.802 --> 00:04:08.401
of copper loss, half of it in the end windings, outside the core.

67
00:04:08.401 --> 00:04:10.481
Iron loss comes on top;

68
00:04:10.481 --> 00:04:12.925
without a rotor, our model leaves it out.

69
00:04:13.841 --> 00:04:19.188
In a water jacket, water and glycol at about 65 degrees, 6 to 10

70
00:04:19.188 --> 00:04:22.941
litres a minute, run through channels in the housing around the core.

71
00:04:23.985 --> 00:04:26.641
Follow the heat from a slot to the water.

72
00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:31.697
It crosses the slot wall, that is the enamel, the resin and the liner;

73
00:04:31.697 --> 00:04:33.425
then tooth and yoke;

74
00:04:33.425 --> 00:04:36.081
then the contact between core and housing;

75
00:04:36.081 --> 00:04:38.077
then the aluminium, into the water.

76
00:04:39.089 --> 00:04:44.255
For the slot loss alone, our one-dimensional estimate puts 13 kelvin on the slot

77
00:04:44.255 --> 00:04:49.681
wall, 12 on the steel, 11 on the contact and 11 on the water:

78
00:04:49.681 --> 00:04:50.845
47 in all.

79
00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:55.864
The contact costs almost as much as the slot wall, and is the least

80
00:04:55.864 --> 00:04:56.913
known:

81
00:04:56.913 --> 00:05:03.625
published models treat it as a film of still air 0.006 to 0.1 millimetres

82
00:05:03.625 --> 00:05:08.797
thick, which moves our 47 kelvin between 38 and 79.

83
00:05:09.969 --> 00:05:14.588
With the jacket alone, the end-winding heat must flow back along the copper into

84
00:05:14.588 --> 00:05:20.439
the slots, and in our estimate the copper reaches about 170 degrees at the

85
00:05:20.439 --> 00:05:22.205
far ends of the end windings.

86
00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:28.136
So many hairpin motors also spray oil on the end windings, about 2 litres

87
00:05:28.136 --> 00:05:30.225
a minute in one test.

88
00:05:30.225 --> 00:05:34.065
Per square metre, oil takes far less heat than water:

89
00:05:34.065 --> 00:05:39.985
100 to 250 watts per kelvin, against about 1,400.

90
00:05:39.985 --> 00:05:44.534
But the end windings offer five times the core's outer surface, with nothing in

91
00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:45.334
between.

92
00:05:45.905 --> 00:05:51.134
Counting copper loss only, the end windings then stay near 70 degrees and the

93
00:05:51.134 --> 00:05:53.905
slot copper near 110.

94
00:05:53.905 --> 00:05:59.217
For the same 150 degrees, the winding carries half as much current again:

95
00:05:59.217 --> 00:06:03.697
13.5 amps per square millimetre instead of 9.

96
00:06:03.697 --> 00:06:05.661
Now the slot path sets the limit.

97
00:06:06.641 --> 00:06:09.713
Our IEC-frame motor has no liquid:

98
00:06:09.713 --> 00:06:12.945
a shaft fan blows air along its fins.

99
00:06:12.945 --> 00:06:17.857
Even 80 kelvin above the air, they shed only about 350 watts in our

100
00:06:17.857 --> 00:06:18.657
estimate.

101
00:06:23.573 --> 00:06:24.757
Chapter three.

102
00:06:24.757 --> 00:06:26.305
Connections and sensors.

103
00:06:27.761 --> 00:06:31.345
Three leads leave our stator, one per phase.

104
00:06:31.345 --> 00:06:37.169
Each joins its phase's two parallel paths, so it carries twice a path's current.

105
00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:43.345
Our model draws them with the same bar as the pins, 14.15 square millimetres:

106
00:06:43.345 --> 00:06:47.709
when the winding carries 10 amps per square millimetre, the lead carries 20.

107
00:06:48.625 --> 00:06:54.033
Production stators use stamped copper bars, one per phase and one for the neutral,

108
00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:57.329
moulded into a plastic carrier over the weld side;

109
00:06:57.329 --> 00:07:00.049
we found no published cross-section.

110
00:07:00.049 --> 00:07:03.997
To match the winding, our leads would need 28 square millimetres.

111
00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:08.369
The neutral ring, by contrast, needs no more copper.

112
00:07:08.369 --> 00:07:14.929
Its six path ends sit in the order U, W, V, U, W, V,

113
00:07:14.929 --> 00:07:17.949
so no stretch of it carries more than one path's current.

114
00:07:19.057 --> 00:07:20.785
Where do the leads go?

115
00:07:20.785 --> 00:07:25.623
Our IEC-frame motor brings six leads to a box on top, with links for

116
00:07:25.623 --> 00:07:27.313
star or delta.

117
00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:31.637
The EV motor has its star inside, so three leads rise out of the

118
00:07:31.637 --> 00:07:35.645
end, to an axial box or straight through the wall into the inverter.

119
00:07:36.721 --> 00:07:40.177
Two sensors complete the stator.

120
00:07:40.177 --> 00:07:43.910
A thermistor, a resistor that changes with temperature, sits on the neutral ring or

121
00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:45.617
in the end winding.

122
00:07:45.617 --> 00:07:49.568
In one study the hot spot ran 50 kelvin ahead of it after a

123
00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:52.733
load step, so the controller adds a thermal model.

124
00:07:53.649 --> 00:07:58.923
And a resolver, a small rotary transformer on the shaft, tells the inverter where

125
00:07:58.923 --> 00:08:00.753
the rotor's poles are.

126
00:08:00.753 --> 00:08:03.869
For 8 poles its signal repeats four times a turn.

127
00:08:08.971 --> 00:08:10.123
Chapter four.

128
00:08:10.123 --> 00:08:10.923
The rotor.

129
00:08:12.487 --> 00:08:15.943
The rotor and its magnets get lectures of their own;

130
00:08:15.943 --> 00:08:20.391
here we only fit one, sized from published motors.

131
00:08:20.391 --> 00:08:25.769
One teardown of a 48-slot, 8-pole motor with a core of our diameter found

132
00:08:25.769 --> 00:08:32.071
an air gap, the clearance between rotor and stator, of 0.89 millimetres.

133
00:08:32.071 --> 00:08:36.391
Others report 0.55 to 0.73.

134
00:08:36.391 --> 00:08:37.843
We take 0.7.

135
00:08:38.791 --> 00:08:43.751
Its magnets are magnetised before it goes in, and they pull on the core:

136
00:08:43.751 --> 00:08:48.604
B squared over two mu-nought per unit area, with B the flux density in

137
00:08:48.604 --> 00:08:52.647
the gap and mu-nought the permeability of air.

138
00:08:52.647 --> 00:08:57.831
At the 0.9 tesla we assume, 320 kilopascals.

139
00:08:57.831 --> 00:09:00.423
Centred, the pulls cancel;

140
00:09:00.423 --> 00:09:05.424
with the rotor against one side, we estimate some 5 kilonewtons, the weight of

141
00:09:05.424 --> 00:09:06.224
half a tonne.

142
00:09:07.015 --> 00:09:09.639
So the rotor never goes in by hand:

143
00:09:09.639 --> 00:09:14.567
a machine holds rotor and stator on one axis with guide sleeves.

144
00:09:14.567 --> 00:09:18.938
A touch could chip a magnet or break its coating, and one simulation finds

145
00:09:18.938 --> 00:09:21.683
the pull largest just as the rotor enters.

146
00:09:22.631 --> 00:09:27.802
Two ball bearings carry the shaft, at about 18,000 revolutions a minute in one

147
00:09:27.802 --> 00:09:29.511
recent car.

148
00:09:29.511 --> 00:09:34.554
The inverter can drive small sparks through their grease, so many motors use insulated

149
00:09:34.554 --> 00:09:37.043
or ceramic bearings, or a grounding ring.

150
00:09:38.023 --> 00:09:42.520
Once the motor is closed the gap cannot be measured, so the bearing seats

151
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.231
must keep it even:

152
00:09:44.231 --> 00:09:50.291
a repair-shop rule asks within 10 percent of the mean, 0.07 millimetres for ours.

153
00:09:51.175 --> 00:09:56.808
Our IEC-frame motor has a die-cast cage rotor with no magnets, so there is

154
00:09:56.808 --> 00:09:57.895
no pull:

155
00:09:57.895 --> 00:10:01.651
it slides in by hand and runs in two small ball bearings.

156
00:10:02.714 --> 00:10:03.930
Chapter five.

157
00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.094
End-of-line test.

158
00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.206
Every finished motor is tested again.

159
00:10:09.206 --> 00:10:13.775
The insulation tests of lecture five are repeated, and the sealed water jacket is

160
00:10:13.775 --> 00:10:15.382
filled with helium:

161
00:10:15.382 --> 00:10:20.962
one maker of leak testers allows about 0.06 cubic centimetres a minute to escape.

162
00:10:21.846 --> 00:10:25.942
Then a drive motor spins it, its own terminals open.

163
00:10:25.942 --> 00:10:32.470
The magnets induce a voltage, the back-EMF, growing with speed, turns and flux.

164
00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:38.011
At our assumed 0.9 tesla, our 16 turns should give about 28 volts r.m.s.

165
00:10:38.011 --> 00:10:41.442
between two leads per thousand revolutions a minute.

166
00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.942
The three phases must match each other and the design.

167
00:10:45.942 --> 00:10:50.358
Back-EMF finds a weak, missing or wrongly fitted magnet;

168
00:10:50.358 --> 00:10:53.442
in one study it hardly saw an off-centre rotor.

169
00:10:54.326 --> 00:10:56.822
The same spin sets the resolver:

170
00:10:56.822 --> 00:11:01.116
the tester shifts its zero until it lines up with the rotor's magnets, to

171
00:11:01.116 --> 00:11:03.298
about half a degree of the electrical cycle.

172
00:11:04.118 --> 00:11:06.838
Last, noise and vibration.

173
00:11:06.838 --> 00:11:11.702
Sensors on the housing track orders, tones at fixed multiples of the speed:

174
00:11:11.702 --> 00:11:17.782
our 48 slots give a 4.8-kilohertz whine at 6,000 revolutions a minute.

175
00:11:17.782 --> 00:11:20.054
Limits are learnt from good motors;

176
00:11:20.054 --> 00:11:25.730
in one study, a rotor 0.2 millimetres off centre changed single orders strongly.

177
00:11:26.646 --> 00:11:30.998
Our IEC-frame motor has no magnets, so no back-EMF:

178
00:11:30.998 --> 00:11:34.722
it runs at no load, and its current and losses are compared instead.

179
00:11:35.837 --> 00:11:36.637
Summary.

180
00:11:36.605 --> 00:11:37.417
Four points.

181
00:11:38.521 --> 00:11:39.321
One.

182
00:11:39.289 --> 00:11:42.905
A shrink fit holds the stator, sized for heat:

183
00:11:42.905 --> 00:11:46.181
in our model the torque needs only about a sixth of it.

184
00:11:47.001 --> 00:11:47.833
Two.

185
00:11:47.833 --> 00:11:52.121
Heat crosses the slot wall, the steel and the contact to the water;

186
00:11:52.121 --> 00:11:56.805
in our estimate, oil on the end windings adds half as much current again.

187
00:11:57.721 --> 00:11:58.553
Three.

188
00:11:58.553 --> 00:12:01.273
Each lead carries both parallel paths;

189
00:12:01.273 --> 00:12:04.165
a thermistor and a resolver complete the stator.

190
00:12:04.985 --> 00:12:05.785
Four.

191
00:12:05.785 --> 00:12:10.642
The magnet rotor goes in on a guide against kilonewtons of pull, by our

192
00:12:10.642 --> 00:12:13.477
estimate, and every motor is tested again.

193
00:12:14.329 --> 00:12:18.029
That is the end of lecture six, and of our lectures on the hairpin

194
00:12:18.029 --> 00:12:19.193
stator.

195
00:12:19.193 --> 00:12:24.970
Flat wire became pins, the pins a welded, impregnated stator, and the stator a

196
00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:25.770
motor.

197
00:12:26.417 --> 00:12:27.825
Next in this course:

198
00:12:27.825 --> 00:12:32.671
the rotor lectures, on magnets and how rotors are made, and the design lectures

199
00:12:32.671 --> 00:12:35.281
that size the stator you have seen built.

200
00:12:35.281 --> 00:12:39.197
Meanwhile, the free Viewer lets you take it apart, pin by pin.

201
00:12:39.857 --> 00:12:45.809
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

202
00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:47.197
motoreka.my.

